WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.360
Although the suffix tree approach has&nbsp;
reduced total run time and memory for read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.720
mapping significantly, it still possesses a great&nbsp;
challenge in memory as the genome string is huge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.160
Can we reduce the genome string even smaller?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.840
How can we compress the information in the genome&nbsp;
to allow efficient multiple pattern matching?&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.840
One way to compress the genome string is by&nbsp;
representing the number of repeats in numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.080
For example, given the genome string here,&nbsp;
instead of writing G 10 times, we can simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:42.480
compress the information and write it as “10G”.&nbsp;
Similarly, instead of writing "C" 11 times, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.800
can just write "11C". If the genome has a lot of&nbsp;
repeats like this, it would be easy to compress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.200
However, the problem is that the genomes don't&nbsp;
have lots of repeats like this. Another way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.840
which can compress the information in the genome&nbsp;
is to use the Burrows-Wheeler Transformation.&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.080
Burrows-Wheeler Transform is&nbsp;
a way to sort and group data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.320
so that similar letters are grouped together&nbsp;
so that it's easier to compress the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.880
For example, the word “BANANA”. After&nbsp;
Burrows-Wheeler Transform you get the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.560
NNBAAA. Because the “As” are grouped together&nbsp;
it is now much easier to compress them.&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.040
How does it works? First, we keep moving the&nbsp;
last letter to the front to create a table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.384
For example, the word “BANANA”, we move the&nbsp;
last letter “A” to the front to make “ABANAN”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.800
We repeat this again and&nbsp;
move the “N” to the front.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.800
We repeat this until the first&nbsp;
letter is at the last position.&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.320
The second step is we sort the table, starting&nbsp;
from a left column and we get “AAABNN”. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.800
“A” on the first column are the same, then we&nbsp;
compare the second column where “B” comes before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.680
“N”. Then the third column and fourth column and&nbsp;
so on and so forth. Then we have the sorted table.&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.040
The third step is to take the last column&nbsp;
as the Burrows-Wheeler Transform output.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.120
In this case, the output is “NNBAAA”. Note that&nbsp;
the original string “BANANA” is at row number 4.&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.960
Why do we need to go through so many steps to&nbsp;
rearrange the letters, sort and create the matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.880
table? One of the advantage of Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;
Transform is that, it allows the genome strings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.320
to be compressed and decompressed to get back&nbsp;
the original genome strings. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.520
if we use the regular sorting algorithm,&nbsp;
the word “BANANA” will turn into “AAABNN”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.200
The problem with this is that it is impossible&nbsp;
for us to retrieve the original words “BANANA”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.920
Burrows-Wheeler Transform is&nbsp;
different. We can reverse the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.840
compressed information back to the original&nbsp;
words by just remembering the row number.&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.000
There are 5 steps to invert the Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;
Transformed string back to its original message.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:03.120
First, sort the output into alphabetical order.&nbsp;
For example, the output of Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:12.800
Transform of “BANANA” is “NNBAAA”. After sorting,&nbsp;
it became “AAABNN”. The second step is to add the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:19.200
output column in front of the sorted column. Then,&nbsp;
in step 3, we sort both columns alphabetically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.720
After sorting, we add the Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;
Transform output in front of the sorted column.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.800
In step 4, we repeat step 2 and 3 again until&nbsp;
the length of the string has the same number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.920
of the column. Finally, you will get the&nbsp;
original string “BANANA” in row number 4.&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.600
In summary, Burrows-Wheeler Transform is a&nbsp;
lossless compression algorithm that offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.840
both compression of the string and retrieval&nbsp;
of the original string whenever it is needed.

